{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792351312,"nonce":"nLockrUpX5ce0NuZudmDccKlLdo=","title":"On macOS the window icon does not change the Dock icon","message":"**What happens**\n\n`WindowAttributes.icon` and `window_set_icon` hand the pixels to winit's `with_window_icon` and `set_window_icon`, which macOS ignores: a window has no icon there, the application does. An app run through Ash (or any host that is not a bundle) shows the host's icon in the Dock, whatever icon the window was given. Reported by RayDE, which passes a 256 px RGBA icon and packages an .icns only for the .app.\n\n**What it should do**\n\nOn macOS, set the application's icon image from the same RGBA pixels, at window creation and in `window_set_icon`; an empty buffer restores the default. Nothing changes elsewhere.\n\n**Plan**\n\nA small crate, xwindow-appicon, with `set(rgba, width, height)`: `NSApplication.setApplicationIconImage` with an `NSImage` made from the pixels, on the main thread, a no-op off macOS, as xwindow-clipboard is built. The native template calls it where it sets the winit icon. hlwindow gets the dependency and the new pin.","files":null}]}